    "description": "A regulatory reporting service generates quarterly disclosure documents. A report is assembled from a mandatory header and summary, plus an arbitrary combination of optional sections: per-entity tables, footnotes, risk annexes and audit trails. Sections depend on the reporting regime, so the same report is produced in several shapes. The current constructor takes 19 positional arguments, half of them optional booleans, and callers regularly pass them in the wrong order; two teams worked around it by copying a private helper and now the validation rules differ. The finance team wants to compose a report step by step, validate at the end that all mandatory sections are present, and reuse the same assembly recipe for a variant. The decision to implement: how to construct these complex documents step by step, with named steps and a single validation point, without a 19-argument constructor.",
    "decision_statement": "Assemble the report through explicit named steps that validate completeness before the document is handed out.",
    "notes": "parameter-object and fluent-interface are the two acceptable supporting axes (data clump + readability)."
  },
